<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uesugi Norimasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1523">1523</a>–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uesugi Norimasa was the Kantō kanrei, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s deputy for eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, from the Yamanouchi branch of the Uesugi family. Defeated by the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-kawagoe-castle">Battle of Kawagoe</a> in 1546, he lost his base in Kōzuke and fled to Echigo, to his family's former deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Nagao Kagetora</a>. In 1561 he adopted Kagetora, passing on the Uesugi name and the office of kanrei to the future Uesugi Kenshin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-norimasa.jpg" alt="Uesugi Norimasa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_of_Uesugi_Norimasa.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Norimasa was the son of Uesugi Norifusa, who died when the boy was only three years old. His adopted brother, Uesugi Norihiro, succeeded their father as head of the clan. When Norihiro was banished in 1531, Norimasa inherited the title of Kantō Kanrei, the shōgun's deputy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kantō region</a>, as a daimyō of the Yamanouchi branch of the Uesugi clan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-kawagoe-castle">Battle of Kawagoe</a> the Ōgigayatsu and Yamanouchi branches of the Uesugi were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>. In 1547 Norimasa was beaten again, this time by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> at the Battle of Odaihara, and his campaigns went from bad to worse. In 1551 Hōjō Ujiyasu defeated him once more at the siege of Hirai castle, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo Province</a> to seek refuge with his vassal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Nagao Kagetora</a>.</p><p>Kagetora agreed to shelter and protect his lord only on conditions: Norimasa had to name him his heir and grant him the titles of Lord of Echigo and Kantō Kanrei. In 1559 Norimasa urged him to win back the Kantō from the Hōjō. From the autumn of 1560 to the summer of 1561 Kagetora campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a>, taking castles such as Numata and Umayabashi and ending with the first siege of Odawara Castle in Sagami Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1561 Norimasa passed the leadership of the Yamanouchi Uesugi and the post of Kantō Kanrei to Kagetora, who took the name Uesugi Terutora to mark his new lineage before settling on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>; as Kenshin he became one of the best-known warlords in Japanese history. Norimasa died on 13 April 1579 during the Otate power struggle, killed by the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu">Uesugi Kagekatsu</a> together with Doumanmaru, the eldest son of Uesugi Kagetora.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uesugi Norimasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1561 the fugitive Kanto deputy <b>Uesugi Norimasa</b> adopted him, giving him the Uesugi name and office.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
